# Isolated CS8409 lost-setup recovery candidate

Prepared on 2026-11-03 for `7.0.14-mbp14342p1`; uninstalled and unloaded.
Based on [PR 220](https://github.com/davidjo/snd_hda_macbookpro/pull/220), pinned
to `b8bc4d935335a9a5476c478d491adf9b18012f58`, applied without fuzz to the exact
kernel-7 companion-driver source based on fork commit `53dd5fa`.

## Behavior and local changes

The existing Apple init is mostly empty, and resume calls codec init/regmap sync.
The candidate reads the GPIO mask: zero is treated as lost setup, then the
existing model-specific boot setup and playback/capture state are restored.
Nonzero masks, including an unreadable all-ones response, do not trigger replay.
This avoids an unconditional reinitialization on every runtime resume.

The upstream patch is mapped from `patch_cirrus/` to `codecs/cirrus/`. A separate
local error patch changes redo to return failures, treats a still-zero mask as
EIO, clears block_unsol on all returned paths, and propagates init errors through
resume instead of unconditionally returning success. It adds no notifier, new
mutex, retry loop, other PR or build-flag change. Existing boot-setup internals
can still log problems without returning an error; this hardening does not make
their complete hardware sequence transactional or time-bounded.

An offline harness compiles the actual lost-setup/init/resume functions against
mocked HDA calls. It covers healthy boot/runtime resume, unreadable response,
successful one-time recovery, setup failure and still-blank failure, including
event unblocking, state resets, preserved unsolicited list and error propagation.
No hardware or PM operation is performed by that harness.

## Review limits

The author reports MacBookPro14,3 S3 recovery on kernel 6.18.49; this is not local
runtime evidence. Normal local codec state passively showed GPIO enable mask
0x27 and the matching 0x106b3900 subsystem. We did not capture that mask in the
failed post-S3 state, so loss of setup remains an unconfirmed local mechanism.

The low-level I2C/amp/CS42L83 setup code remains the existing driver implementation.
Runtime PM, unsolicited-event handling, headphone/microphone state and existing
poll/wait behavior need normal-boot/idle validation before any separately
authorized power test. No new I2C mutex is taken: runtime resume recursion and
deadlock constraints must not be hidden by adding a lock around this path.

## Package and rollback

Build/package only with `python3 experiments/kernel-42/analysis/package-resume-drivers.py audio`.
It creates signed `mbp143-audio-resume-7.0.14-mbp14342p1` 0.1-1, containing only
snd-hda-codec-cs8409.ko, an exact-kernel override, documentation and index/initramfs
hooks. It does not ship the rebuilt common HDA helper: its exported CRCs must
match the kernel. Original trial audio and all T1 modules remain intact.

Future installation requires baseline backup and selection/initramfs checks,
then a planned normal reboot. No live codec reload, reset, raw HDA verb or PM
transition is authorized here. Rollback would remove only this new package,
verify selection of the preserved trial module and regenerate initramfs before
a planned reboot. The official -42 fallback is unaffected. Keep all five PM
records, sleep block and T1 skip guard; do not rearm any old experiment.

Validate native 44.1 kHz formats at safe file amplitude for later audio tests.
Direct hardware playback bypasses normal volume controls. Wi-Fi and audio
packages are independent and must not be combined into one attribution test.
